= main =
Contextual definition is a method for defining terms by 
Belief that some words/phrases have no meaning in isolation but only as parts of
whole statements.

It marks a shift from taking (some) terms, or words as being the primary bearers
of meaning (see [[aristotelian_logic#Categorematic]] to regarding propositions
as the fundamental bearers of meaning 
It is reasonable to ask what import this shift has, for if sentence meanings
are a function of the combination of the words they contain then the meaning of
a word may be recognise as whatever it is that it contributes to sentence
meanings. The difference cashes out in the validity of contextual definition.

=== history ===
A distinction between terms/phrases that have meaning in
isolation and those that don't be traced back as far as the scholastic
account of syncategorematic terms (see [[aristotelian_logic#categorematic and syncategorematic terms]])

Jeremy Bentham first made this shift claiming that:
“if nothing less than the import of an entire proposition be sufficient for the
giving full expression to any the most simple thought, it follows that no word,
being anything more than a fragment of a proposition, no word is in itself the
complete sign of any thought.”

The technique of contextual definition was first explicitly applied by Frege;
but it

brought to the attention of 20th century philosophers
is primarily associated with Russell who used the technique to
great effect in the ground breaking paper _On Denoting_ (see [[russellian_semantics]])

At times Russell talks of contextual definition as showing that the phrases
being contextually defined have no meaning in isolation.
    “C(a man)” means “‘C(x) and x is human’ is not always false”.
    This leaves “a man”, by itself, wholly destitute of meaning, but gives a
    meaning to every proposition in whose verbal expression “a man” occurs.

    According to the view which I advocate, a denoting phrase is essentially
    part of a sentence, and does not, like most single words, have any
    significance on its own account.
								[[local:zz.texts/russell1905-onDenoting.pdf]]

At other times he claims that these phrases are synonymous with the (incomplete)
structures that replace them:
    The names that we commonly use, like “Socrates”, are really abbreviations
    for descriptions...
				[[local:zz.texts/russell1918-thePhilosophyOfLogicalAtomism.pdf]]
	
    “Apollo” means really “the object having such and such properties”. say “the
    object having the properties enumerated in the Classical Dictionary”
												_Principia Mathematica_, 1910-13

