According to Quine(()), John Tooke (1736-1812) attempted to present an etymology according
to which these terms were derived from sensible actions or states (
“if” was a degenerate form of “give”, “but” of “be out”. 



Bentham called this paraphrasis and used the technique primarily to avoid
objects and properties which could not be reduced to empirically acceptable
concepts in their own right.

Bentham's primary target was to reconcile talk of rights with legal positivism.
His move was not to try to define rights in empirically acceptable terms, not to
give a phrase or term that could replace “has the right to” in sentences, or
conditions under which rights had been positively conferred, but rather to give
replacements of the whole sentence. In this way talk of rights was to be
replaced with talk of duties imposed upon other members of society, and then
talk of duties was to be replaced with talk of punishment for failure to carry
them out.

Note that this is not the same as giving an analysis of right - for instance
“god's permission” or “requirement of human dignity”, those whether you
like them as analyses or not are replacing phrases, wheras contextual
definitions of paraphrasis is a technique of replacing whole sentneces which
synonyms which avoid troublesome phrases and do not contain any part that is
synonymous to those phrases.




